{
  "headline": "Advance voter turnout increases for 2026 municipal election in Toronto, Canada",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Advance voting for Toronto’s 2026 municipal election saw 222,550 eligible voters participate during a six-day period that ended at 7 p.m. on October 11, 2026. According to the City of Toronto, this turnout represents the highest in over a decade and is a nearly 72 per cent increase compared to the 129,000 early votes recorded during the 2023 mayoral byelection. Election day is scheduled for October 26, 2026, at which time voters must visit their assigned voting places as indicated on their Voter Information Card or the Toronto Elections webpage. Voters who requested mail-in ballots were required to return completed packages to Toronto Elections by noon on October 14, 2026, with the city advising the use of 25 secure, indoor, CCTV-monitored yellow drop boxes located in each ward. Additionally, the deadline for third party advertisers to register to spend money promoting or opposing candidates was 4:30 p.m. on October 9, 2026. Registrations for these advertisers must be filed in person at the Toronto Elections City Hall office."
    },
    {
      "text": "A survey from Liaison Strategies conducted during the advance voting period suggests incumbent Mayor Olivia Chow leads the mayoral race by five points, polling at 51 per cent among decided and leaning voters, while Councillor Brad Bradford polls at 46 per cent. In response to the turnout and polling data, Chow’s team credited the results to the efforts of volunteers campaigning across the city. Conversely, Laryssa Waler, a spokesperson for Bradford’s campaign, stated that the high early turnout indicates voters are making up their minds ahead of the October 26 election day."
    }
  ]
}

